From 3372560c78c84332603c2170cb6c170c6482c769 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 01:06:38 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent_sandbox, git: a repository a jailed agent can write
 runs none of its own drivers
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A jail keeps the agent away from the host, but the project it works in
stays writable, all but <root>/.git and <root>/.zed (0057's two read-only
maps, unchanged by 0141). The security audit of 2026-11-21 (lead 1, PROPOSAL
§6.61 in the hub) asked whether that is enough, and on 2026-11-26 a probe in
project's integration tests answered no: with a project open, plain
processes standing in for the agent ran `git init sub`, set
`filter.x.clean = touch <marker>; cat`, wrote `* filter=x` into
.gitattributes and changed a file, and the editor's own scan registered
`sub` and ran `git status` in it -- running the filter on the host, outside
the jail, with no click. GitBinary overrides hooks, ssh, credential helpers
and external diff for an untrusted repository, but never filter or diff
drivers, and a nested repository inherits its project's trust anyway.

The operator chose on 2026-11-26 (options 1 and 3 of §6.61):

  - git: every repository a jailed agent can write (AgentReach::Writable)
    runs untrusted whatever the worktree trust says, with every filter and
    diff driver its own config defines switched off, and with commit
    signing pinned to git's own programs (gpg.program, gpg.ssh.program,
    gpg.x509.program, gpg.ssh.defaultKeyCommand);
  - the jailed root's own .git (AgentReach::RootGitDir) keeps its trust
    but still has its own drivers switched off. That is option 3's case:
    the jail cannot stop the agent from creating <root>/.git when the
    project had none, nor from writing a file that config includes. Option
    3 as first proposed -- create and map an empty <root>/.git -- was not
    built: the worktree scanner takes the first `.git` it finds as the
    repository root, so a placeholder would hide the enclosing repository
    of every project opened as a subfolder.

How a driver is switched off: `-c filter.<name>.clean= -c
filter.<name>.smudge= -c filter.<name>.process= -c
filter.<name>.required=false` and `-c diff.<name>.textconv= -c
diff.<name>.command=`, the empty command being git's own "no driver" (the
Claude Code VS Code extension does the same before `git worktree add`).
The names come from `git config --includes --show-scope --name-only
--get-regexp '^(filter|diff)\..+\.(clean|smudge|process|textconv|command)$'`,
run before each command; only the `local` and `worktree` scopes count, and
a file either includes reports their scope (measured on git 2.56.1, with
the include in the working tree). Global and system config are the user's
own and are left alone, so a globally installed LFS filter still runs. A
driver name -c cannot address (it holds `=` or a newline), or a listing
that fails, refuses the command: GIT_DIR points at a path that does not
exist, so git stops before reading the tree. What it costs: in a jailed
project, a file such a driver converts (an LFS pointer from a local filter
definition, a git-crypt blob) reads as modified in Zeo's git views. Merge
drivers are not screened: git runs one only for a merge the user starts.

Which repositories: agent_sandbox::git_reach keeps the jailed project
roots (launch::project_root of every open local project whose jail
effective_enabled says is on) and their writable folders, each as written
and canonical, current on every settings change and worktree add/remove,
so it holds before the agent's first launch too -- a repository planted in
an earlier session is screened from the first `git status`. It registers
project::git_store::GitAgentReach, a gpui Global; LocalRepositoryState::new
hands its check to the backend (GitRepository::set_agent_reach_check, a
no-op default for backends that run no git binary), and the backend asks it
on every command, so turning the jail on or off needs no reopen. A
repository is Writable when its working or common directory is under a
writable folder, or under a jailed root without its common directory being
that root's own .git; RootGitDir when it is; the widest reach wins when two
jailed roots nest.

Tests: git -- the listing parser (local/worktree only, a dotted name kept
whole, an unaddressable name refused) and, against real git, a fixture
whose filter and textconv touch markers: with no check both run (the
baseline), Writable and RootGitDir run neither with the drivers in
.git/config or in an included working-tree file, and an unaddressable name
refuses the command. agent_sandbox -- classify (root .git, nested, a .git
file pointing outside, a look-alike prefix, a bare git dir in the project,
a writable folder, nested roots) and, end to end with RealFs, the probe's
fixture: the filter runs with the jail off (the control) and does not with
it on. Red seen: with the screen switched off three git tests fail, and
without git_reach::init the end-to-end jailed test fails.
---

